<!--END MULTISELECT -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why \((2,2)\) satisfies the cost constraint but not the length constraint.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>2 yards of each color costs $44, which is less than $110, so it satisfies this constraint. However, this is only 4 yards altogether, which is not at least 9.5 yards.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Find at least one pair of numbers that satisfies both constraints. Be prepared to explain how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\((10,1)\), \((8,2)\), and (\(6.5,3.5)\) (any point that is in the intersection of the two inequalities).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What does the pair of numbers represent in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Yoru answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\((10,1)\) means 10 yards of light fabric and 1 yard of dark fabric. This is 11 yards of fabric altogether, which is more than 9.5. It costs $103 because \(9(10)+1(13)=103\). This is less than the $110 maximum.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the two inequalities together as a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">system of inequalities</term>.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}9x+13y\leq110\\x+y\geq9.5\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
